A||||10||DecadenceR||Amp Mounting||||1||
Z||000000||DecadenceR||06-08-2001||10:09 AM||||OKay, this could just be me, but I'm slightly concerned with the way some people have their amps mounted.<BR>As most people know, heat is an amps worst enemy. That's why they're usually made from aluminum and have fans and all that (better ones anyway). To dissipate the heat.<BR>Now, a lot of people here have talked about mounting the amp in the trunk. Why???<BR>First there is no air flow through the trunk at all. Second, what's mounted right on the other side of the trunk, with no much between them? Yes ladies and gentlemen, your muffler. <BR>I had a socket sitting on the small lip in the trunk up against the motor side wall. I drove from work to home, which is about 30 minutes. Then i remembered the tools in my trunk and decided to take them inside. And that socket was REALLY hot.<BR>Anyway, to sum up my long overstated point...<BR>In the best interests of the money spent on your equipment, and the sound quality, I would advise finding another place to mount your amp. IMHO, it'll be much easier on the life of the amp, and then you won't have to worry about your amp cutting off from heat.<P>Ok, I'm done.||||reg||1
Z||000001||stimpy||06-08-2001||10:28 AM||||And you would suggest? I agree that mounting in rear trunk is not a great idea, but people gotta put it somewhere and there ain't no wide open, well ventilated clean dry places to mount amps in our little cars. Mine are behind the passenger seat, and I know that's not the best spot either. What's left?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Slammed Fiero||06-08-2001||10:36 AM||||I have only ever seen a few people put it in the trunk , Most put it behind the driver seat. I have 2 amps behind my passenger seat 1 powers the deck , the other is going to power the sub as soon as I figure out where to put it. <P>Mine gets a bit hot , but nothing bad ,it has a temp sensor that will shut it down if it gets near overheating. I have never had a problem with it in the year it's been there.<P><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">     <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals..Autocrossed , Tracked , abused.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DecadenceR||06-08-2001||11:53 AM||||Well behind the seats in the cabin is definitely better than in the trunk. While it may not be the best ventilated area, it's a lot better than being in a sealed trunk with that much heat.<BR>One idea I have is for the front where the spare tire is. That's pretty sealed too, but a lot less heat and you'd be able to port it to get some free air in there as well.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||06-08-2001||01:10 PM||||Interesting....Ya know, my Premier amps have NEVER overheated in my trunk, even with a 3.4L Camaro engine w/headers. I think some people miss the point. Anyone have an amp in their trunk with an actual temp sensor, that gives you a digital reading? Most class A/B amps run extremly hot (not to the touch, but grab the heatsink and see how long you can stand it![dont actually do this BTW, it hurts]), even hotter then what you would find temps to be in the trunk of a fiero. Pure class D (subwoofer only) amps run more efficiently, so generate less heat(all mine a A/B class). Now, the reason my amps don't overheat is because of the enclosure. They are sealed under a peice of clear Lexon on the top, and 3/4 MDF on the sides and back, all covered with thermo-insulation. On each side of the 2 amp racks, there a 2 fans. One side pushes air in, other side pulls the air out. Even blowing hot air across the amp(s) cools it efficiently for prolonged life and proper operation. This is really a space issue in the fiero, so trunk mounting provides a fair alternative. Seeing as some (me among others) have subs mounted in the front of their cars, some have subs mounted behind their seats, but no one mounts subs in their trunks, so it works well for amps. The best way to mount your amps is flat, top up of course. Make sure you have adaquate ventalation if you mount them vertical, and never mount them upside-down, it will kill them in a matter of days.<P>BTW, if your trunk is getting that hot, you might have other problems. Make sure you don't have exhaust problems first off, make sure the heat shield for the muffler is in place. My trunk on either of my Fiero's never gets that hot.<P>Ask me anything, I could ramblem on about this all day if thats not already obvious.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||06-08-2001||01:12 PM||||One other thing. Behind the seats mounting of the amps is just as good as trunk mounting. Like I said, it all depends on how the amp rack is set up. I would imagine your behind the seat amps run hotter then my trunk amps if tested...||||reg||1
Z||000006||The Fieromaster||06-08-2001||01:29 PM||||.<p>[This message has been edited by The Fieromaster (edited 06-08-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000007||The Fieromaster||06-08-2001||01:29 PM||||My buddy Fieromaster88 runs his in his trunk... His never cuts out or over heats and it sounds AWESOME!  Amps with fans and coolant systems arent any better there just less efficient!  If you have a 2 500 watt monoblock amps and one needs a fan to stay cool that amp isnt very good cause it produces too much heat per output... There is a EVIL sony amp here that is 2,000 watts RMS! and it doesnt use one fan!  Cause it produces the power efficiently!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.askfelons.com/fieromaster/fieromastergif1.gif">   <BR>~In Memory Of Jesse Cesek~ 1980-2000<BR>1985 GT ~Purple, Black and Yellow Interior(Fastback Converted)<BR>1997 Mitsu Eclipse GSX (AWD, Turbo) DOA on 12/10/2000||||reg||10
Z||000008||FieroGTguy||06-08-2001||03:14 PM||||I'm about to mount my Fosgate 360.6 and 250.1 amps together behind the pass seat.  I must mount them sideways.  If you say mounting them upside down will kill them, is it a bad idea mounting them vertically sideways?<P>Greg||||reg||1
Z||000009||Philphine||06-08-2001||04:20 PM||||i'm using two small amps in the front trunk over the spare. i sto....i mean aquired the idea from fierosound when his car was in car stereo review. pretty much where i plan to put them in other fieros too as i build them.||||reg||1
Z||000010||fierofrek||06-08-2001||04:33 PM||||I have 2 650 watt amps under my dash. never had any problems what so ever. no heating problems, no cut out problems. only problem i had was making them fitting properly.||||reg||1
